1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
To solve this bottleneck, the creators of

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:07,950
ZUNA threw out the fixed seating chart.

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:12,791
Instead of forcing the brain's symphony into a rigid grid, what if

4
00:00:12,891 --> 00:00:17,208
we just attached a precise GPS tracker to every single note?

5
00:00:17,333 --> 00:00:21,283
First, ZUNA acts like a digital metronome.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,995
It takes the continuous stream of electrical data from

7
00:00:26,095 --> 00:00:31,125
each channel and slices it into tiny, 0.125-second windows.

8
00:00:31,266 --> 00:00:34,241
Each of these short snapshots is packaged

9
00:00:34,341 --> 00:00:37,091
into a mathematical block, or 'token'.

10
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,919
Next comes the breakthrough. ZUNA stamps every

11
00:00:41,019 --> 00:00:44,075
single token with a 4D coordinate tag.

12
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,468
It records the exact 3D physical location of the

13
00:00:48,568 --> 00:00:52,200
sensor on the scalp—the X, Y, and Z axes—

14
00:00:52,366 --> 00:00:55,447
along with its specific moment in time.

15
00:00:55,547 --> 00:00:59,116
This is called 4D Rotary Positional Encoding.

16
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:04,405
Because of this simple mathematical tag, ZUNA no longer

17
00:01:04,505 --> 00:01:08,623
cares if it is fed 16 channels, 64 channels,

18
00:01:08,723 --> 00:01:12,458
or 256 channels. It just reads the tags.

